  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is no performance reference and no information about which design axes (modality, profile setting, encoder choice, aggregation, fusion) actually matter. The highest-priority uncertainty is establishing a baseline performance level for a multimodal model using standard, well-motivated components. 2) What this tests: config_051 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt, resnet50, concat) is a clean multimodal baseline that uses a learned single-cell RNA encoder (scgpt), a standard image backbone (resnet50), a single profile (eliminating the aggregation variable entirely), and the simplest fusion (concat). It establishes the reference performance for the study's core question—whether combining RNA and image modalities is informative—while keeping every other design choice at its most standard value. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Starting with the simplest reasonable multimodal configuration on the study's main path (rather than a fully complex multiprofile/transformer/weighted setup) means that in subsequent experiments we can add one axis of complexity at a time (e.g., multiprofile + aggregation, a different RNA encoder, a stronger image backbone, weighted fusion) and attribute performance changes to that single change. It also avoids the risk that a poor result is confounded by multiple simultaneously exotic choices. This maximizes the information gained per experiment under the limited budget."
}

